To use a Gaussian filter before applying the Sobel operator, you can add a few lines of code to your existing MATLAB script. Here's the modified code:

```matlab
% Read input image
input_image = imread('image.jpg');

% Convert to grayscale
gray_image = rgb2gray(input_image);

% Apply Gaussian filter
gaussian_filtered_image = imfilter(gray_image, fsme(3,3,'gaussian'));

% Define Sobel operator in x direction
sobel_x = [-1 0 1; -2 0 2; -1 0 1];

% Define Sobel operator in y direction
sobel_y = [-1 -2 -1; 0 0 0; 1 2 1];

% Apply Sobel operator in x direction
edge_image_x = conv2(double(gaussian_filtered_image), sobel_x, 'same');

% Apply Sobel operator in y direction
edge_image_y = conv2(double(gaussian_filtered_image), sobel_y, 'same');

% Combine edges in both directions
edge_image = sqrt(edge_image_x.^2 + edge_image_y.^2);

% Display edge image
imshow(edge_image);
```

In this modified code, we first apply a Gaussian filter to the grayscale image using the `imfilter` function and the `fsme` function to create a Gaussian kernel. The `'gaussian'` string specifies the type of kernel to use.

After applying the Gaussian filter, we proceed with the Sobel edge detection as before, applying the Sobel operators in both the x and y directions and combining the results to obtain the final edge image.

The Gaussian filter helps to reduce noise in the image by smoothing out small variations in the intensity values. This can lead to more accurate edge detection and a cleaner final result.

Feel free to experiment with different kernel sizes and standard deviations for the Gaussian filter to achieve the desired level of noise reduction and edge detection accuracy.

