It seems like the issue you're facing is related to the CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) configuration of your server. When the server sets `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` to `*`, it allows any origin to access your resources, including requests without proper authentication.

To include the authorization header in your requests, you need to ensure that the client (e.g., browser or JavaScript application) sends the authorization header with each request. However, when the server allows any origin to access your resources, it bypasses the client's authentication mechanism, and the authorization header is not sent.

To resolve this issue, you can modify the CORS configuration of your server to allow only trusted origins or to use a more restrictive `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` value. For example, you can set `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` to `https://example.com` to allow requests only from the `https://example.com` domain.

Alternatively, you can use a proxy server or a reverse proxy to handle the requests and add the authorization header before forwarding the requests to your server. This way, the proxy server acts as an intermediary between the client and the server, and it can add the authorization header to the requests based on the client's authentication credentials.

Here's an example of how you can configure Nginx as a proxy server to add the authorization header:
```
server {
    listen 80;
    server_name example.com;

    location /api/ {
        proxy_pass http://your-server-ip:8000/api/;
        proxy_set_header Authorization $http_authorization;
    }
}
```
In this example, the Nginx server listens on port 80 for incoming requests and forwards them to your server at `http://your-server-ip:8000/api/`. The `proxy_set_header` directive adds the `Authorization` header to the requests based on the `$http_authorization` variable, which contains the authorization header from the client's request.

